ue "2{e}^{-t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343057/field-3/2c3e8a789ce1592afadca121f5f56ba7154f00044795647672cfd4a90d3e7791" :answer/id #uuid "7ea966a6-da44-5574-90bb-a2c3a3ff14e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-t}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343057/field-3/de44dd60d5e3334fc55fbcac5c5844e18b62f45fd41cbfc532dcefadae9bb93e" :answer/id #uuid "2b9f6e58-9388-534f-bd3f-def7f32d3ad2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{-t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343057/field-3/1d9e085a2a5aa7339aa4a0e35fc9571f1149d4771c67cb2f36dc88756f019953" :answer/id #uuid "d55f8187-e81d-5c10-94c8-e29021bc7885" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343057/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7fa98f-2bdb-5d90-a00b-fb1963ffa565" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-343057/field-3/e5f2ba150f27e3b5e2f7e19f40d7510fbde3f0aed1621bdfd8206cdc1275e868" :answer-field/choices ["q-343057/field-3/e5f2ba150f27e3b5e2f7e19f40d7510fbde3f0aed1621bdfd8206cdc1275e868" "q-343057/field-3/2c3e8a789ce1592afadca121f5f56ba7154f00044795647672cfd4a90d3e7791" "q-343057/field-3/de44dd60d5e3334fc55fbcac5c5844e18b62f45fd41cbfc532dcefadae9bb93e" "q-343057/field-3/1d9e085a2a5aa7339aa4a0e35fc9571f1149d4771c67cb2f36dc88756f019953"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343057" :question/id #uuid "f44dbcab-4002-5eb8-9303-ab06ab01d7f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343057" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-2)+Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s)-2 & =\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $2$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+1)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2({s}^{2}+1)}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}+s+3}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2{s}^{2}+s+3}{(s+1)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+1)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{s}^{2}+s+3}{(s+1)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2{s}^{2}+s+3=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+1)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+3=A((-1)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,4=2A\\,⟹\\,A=2.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n2=A+B\\,⟹\\,2=2+B\\,⟹\\,B=0.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n3=A+C\\,⟹\\,3=2+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2}{s+1}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} & ={e}^{-t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =2{e}^{-t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-343057/field-1" "q-343057/field-2" "q-343057/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06fedec8-5aed-52f9-bd4f-aa81b9180ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343057"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342962/field-1/b4a99802a9188d77830368251ccd41f46ffe71b8fbc45c1f055d9a4bdc16e513" :answer/id #uuid "7544bdef-51a1-55a0-aa85-8c31da6353b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342962/field-1/4bd4f951086ef81093c610db7bbc64c6ee4bb0bdc1bd4267465e86baaa20f8ea" :answer/id #uuid "f71b6b84-c584-5ef9-a3bb-2245971c7048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s-3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342962/field-1/24b1deaacbc6ddce0f1edef62af81c862fe67a7173adedfba000d98e328fda57" :answer/id #uuid "e7319b6f-eaad-59d1-be37-2283ecdb9052" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3-s}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342962/field-1/133eef9a7889d519acbf2455ef10fd32d9c67e911ba4312144fdc00a117c7707" :answer/id #uuid "86792d2b-60ff-537b-ab67-ac4402ecb5cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342962/field-1/6ea5cf2917774e1c12818af11c3278ac2f3b