eratorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342961/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "32df38cd-b4dd-56f9-857a-82a5d314d067" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342961/field-3/1c3456fd873ad6e9e43adad27d09383a69b41dc5062074592736765b154aed4e" :answer-field/choices ["q-342961/field-3/1c3456fd873ad6e9e43adad27d09383a69b41dc5062074592736765b154aed4e" "q-342961/field-3/d82b9276f122e89b6a7f4cb0b5f9d704fb43a18b23c5245964b2f6e9e3620f62" "q-342961/field-3/a597d54cc9bea29df93ffa58861181794455f3fce6b4d00ee9a8c4e801902fdc" "q-342961/field-3/bb11140d303c1bc338109569242fbc6250e7d08d200cb5f8be4f32e1607813b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342961" :question/id #uuid "2839b85c-1838-5d26-90a6-4ced0d0fbb59" :question/math-academy-id "q-342961" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+1=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-2+1=A((-2)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,-1=5A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{5}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n1=A+2C\\,⟹\\,1=-\\frac{1}{5}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{5})}{s+2}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5}s+\\frac{3}{5})}{{s}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}+\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{3}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342961/field-1" "q-342961/field-2" "q-342961/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "06fedec8-5aed-52f9-bd4f-aa81b9180ad4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342961"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6657"] :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ for $s > 1$ if \n\n$$\ny' + y =  2e^t, \\qquad y(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{2{e}^{t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =2L{{e}^{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{ y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'\\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L \\{e^{at}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L} \\{ e^t \\}  = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =2L{{e}^{t}} \\\\ (sY(s)+1)+Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{s-1}-1 \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{2-(s-1)}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3-s}{{s}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6661"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} + 4y = 3e^{-t}, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/1da481c39504266a4f42e13ef938ff0f7eb75d1174a149bed8d80e47e25d46e6.png)\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/ffa301a263d519d3b653c1e1be9b8e8ef59f3cb2fb515e794c00b6b27dadc762.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/54fb8d20b3cd1205ec6ee7c94b74418e45bc239229159c093dbb34bd908f72fd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} + 4\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = 3\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-t}\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative, $\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0),$ and the standard transform \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\} = \\frac{1}{s-a}, \\qquad s > a,\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-2)+4Y(s) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s)-2 & =\\frac{3}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $2$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+4)Y(s) & =2+\\frac{3}{s+1} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2(s+1)}{s+1}+\\frac{3}{s+1} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2s+5}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s + 4)(s+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2s+5}{(s+4)(s+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{s+4} + \\dfrac{B}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B,$ we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n2s+5 = A(s+1) + B(s+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s = -4,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(-4)+5 = A(-4+1) + B(0) \\implies -3 = -3A \\implies A = 1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s = -1,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(-1)+5 = A(0) + B(-1+4) \\implies 3 = 3B \\implies B = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+4} + \\dfrac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+4}} & ={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\ y(t) & ={e}^{-4t}+{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21862"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} - 2y = -5\\sin t, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad \\mathcal L\\{\\cos\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/476c6ff72b2927d4d86291e5b9816e54f9c7b3fd254b4f8d73de493162a4a4b7.png)\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/8c16e1eed467dbc46fb8698f9a7e2ceff80d47e571506806fe1baf37ec1050ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072504/assets/a728d0243548e724f95b2331697d75972e05774cf9b9a708c0965361d5e75fc8.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} - 2\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = -5\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transform\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{\\sin t\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s^2+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-1)-2Y(s) & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s)-1 & =-\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)Y(s) & =1-\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+1}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-4}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{s^2 - 4}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{(s-2)(s+2)}{(s - 2)(s^2 + 1)} = \\dfrac{s+2}{s^2 + 1}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
